WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.320
One of the quotes from talks you've given, you say,

00:04.320 --> 00:06.080
when you talk about art being scary,

00:06.080 --> 00:08.680
you say anything that makes you stand out,

00:08.680 --> 00:10.200
makes you vulnerable.

00:10.200 --> 00:13.880
And yet, this very effort to say, and yet,

00:13.880 --> 00:16.480
I am here, and I'm going to do this.

00:16.480 --> 00:20.320
And this particular image, it's just the fact

00:20.320 --> 00:23.000
that there is this, you know,

00:23.000 --> 00:28.000
carceral formation of men, white men,

00:28.000 --> 00:30.880
in this image that is like,

00:30.880 --> 00:36.080
it feels like such a win because of the beauty

00:36.080 --> 00:38.440
that's captured in the movement, literally,

00:38.440 --> 00:41.440
in this piece against that backdrop.

00:41.440 --> 00:44.840
Would you read this one to us and maybe describe it visually?

00:44.840 --> 00:46.480
Do you care?

00:46.480 --> 00:50.840
It says, Southern Transresistence is beautiful

00:50.840 --> 00:55.160
by Mickey Bradford against a red background.

00:55.160 --> 01:04.720
And there are three white cops.

01:04.720 --> 01:10.360
And in between them is a young, black trans woman

01:10.360 --> 01:15.880
who's voguing in a loral little dress.

01:15.880 --> 01:20.320
And in the foreground are the adoring

01:20.320 --> 01:28.480
hands and raised fists of the community protesters.

01:28.480 --> 01:31.360
OK, and is the cop fiddling with his ring?

01:31.360 --> 01:32.040
I know.

01:32.040 --> 01:33.280
That is so funny.

01:33.280 --> 01:35.080
Literally in the photo, he looks like he's

01:35.080 --> 01:38.680
anxiously fondling his wedding ring.

01:38.680 --> 01:43.560
Yeah, I mean, this is really just an example

01:43.560 --> 01:47.040
of rapid response art because the week that this happened,

01:47.040 --> 01:48.760
you know, I already was doing this project.

01:48.760 --> 01:51.440
I was in touch with Mickey as an organizer at Song.

01:51.440 --> 01:55.800
We had worked together before we knew about each other's work.

01:55.800 --> 02:02.000
I can't remember the piece of legislation

02:02.000 --> 02:06.960
that passed right now, but it was some anti-trans and queer

02:06.960 --> 02:10.720
legislation that was being passed.

02:10.720 --> 02:12.680
And so there was a protest in front of the,

02:12.680 --> 02:14.520
I believe, the governor's house.

02:14.520 --> 02:18.040
And, you know, they'd just been out there for hours

02:18.040 --> 02:21.600
and finally, you know, not even thinking, oh, this would

02:21.600 --> 02:22.600
be a medium moment.

02:22.600 --> 02:27.240
Like truly, I think, just out of organizing, like,

02:27.240 --> 02:30.120
how can I keep our people energized?

02:30.120 --> 02:36.560
Mickey, who's also a vogueer, just started voguing

02:36.560 --> 02:37.520
in front of the police.

02:37.520 --> 02:41.120
And then it got picked up by the media so much.

02:41.120 --> 02:43.880
And so, and so because we already in touch,

02:43.880 --> 02:47.680
we were able to basically create this overnight.

02:47.800 --> 02:50.680
And release it when there was still media

02:50.680 --> 02:53.040
and public attention.

02:53.040 --> 02:56.360
And just the interplay, I mean, this is the case

02:56.360 --> 03:01.520
with so much of my work, between current events,

03:01.520 --> 03:06.520
rapid response art, social media and the internet,

03:08.880 --> 03:11.080
and action on the ground.

03:11.080 --> 03:12.680
And it's a feedback loop.

